<li class="ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_bibliography"><a href="#bib" title="In SitLLM: Large Language Models for Sitting Posture Health Understanding via Pressure Sensor Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title">References</span></a></li>
</ol></nav>
</nav>
<div class="ltx_page_main">
<div id="infobox" class="infobox">
  <a id="license-tr" href="https://info.arxiv.org/help/license/index.html#licenses-available">
    License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license
  </a>
  <div id="watermark-tr">
arXiv:2509.12994v1 [cs.CL] 16 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">SitLLM: Large Language Models for Sitting Posture Health Understanding via Pressure Sensor Data</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jian Gao
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Fufangchen Zhao
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yiyang Zhang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Danfeng Yan
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Poor sitting posture is a critical yet often overlooked factor contributing to long-term musculoskeletal disorders and physiological dysfunctions. Existing sitting posture monitoring systems, although leveraging visual, IMU, or pressure-based modalities, often suffer from coarse-grained recognition and lack the semantic expressiveness necessary for personalized feedback. In this paper, we propose <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SitLLM</span>, a lightweight multimodal framework that integrates flexible pressure sensing with large language models (LLMs) to enable fine-grained posture understanding and personalized health-oriented response generation. SitLLM comprises three key components: (1) a <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Gaussian-Robust Sensor Embedding Module</span> that partitions pressure maps into spatial patches and injects local noise perturbations for robust feature extraction; (2) a <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Prompt-Driven Cross-Modal Alignment Module</span> that reprograms sensor embeddings into the LLM’s semantic space via multi-head cross-attention using the pre-trained vocabulary embeddings; and (3) a <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Multi-Context Prompt Module</span> that fuses feature-level, structure-level, statistical-level, and semantic-level contextual information to guide instruction comprehension. To enable high-quality cross-modal alignment, we constructed a pressure–text alignment dataset for sitting posture health, comprising 9,255 samples. The dataset was built through a two-stage process combining expert annotation, medical knowledge retrieval, and human–LLM collaborative generation. Extensive experiments demonstrate that SitLLM outperforms existing baselines on multiple sitting health-related tasks, including sitting posture analysis, sitting health-aware question answering, and sitting posture correction suggestions, while maintaining efficiency suitable for edge deployment. Our work highlights the potential of combining low-dimensional sensor modalities with semantically rich LLMs for personalized health monitoring.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id1" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><sup id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">1</sup>Beijing University of Posts and Telecommunications</p>
<p id="p1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">{gaojian, zhaofufangchen, zhangyiyang2025, yandf}@bupt.edu.cn,</p>
</div>
  
</div>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Introduction</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prolonged poor sitting posture has been widely associated with a range of chronic health issues, including cervical spondylosis, lumbar disc herniation, musculoskeletal pain, and compromised cardiopulmonary function <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Robertson et al. 2009</a>)</cite>. Importantly, these health risks often originate not from overall posture but from long-term asymmetry or abnormal pressure on specific body regions such as the neck, lower back, or pelvis. For instance, forward head posture (FHP) alters cervical spine biomechanics by increasing cantilever loads and soft tissue strain, which has been associated with upper cervical joint degeneration and various clinical symptoms including cervicogenic pain and dizziness.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chu, Lo, and Bhaumik 2020</a>)</cite>. Thus, timely detection of localized postural anomalies and targeted feedback is essential for preventing long-term health deterioration and improving the granularity of posture-based health management.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12994v1/introduction.png" id="Sx1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/513;" width="698" height="513" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>SitLLM understands pressure sensor inputs to respond to various posture-related health tasks.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">With increasing awareness of posture-related health concerns, researchers have developed various intelligent sitting posture monitoring systems using visual sensing<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fang et al. 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao and Su 2024</a>)</cite>, inertial measurement units (IMUs)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang et al. 2021</a>)</cite>, and flexible pressure sensors<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yoon, Moon, and Son 2024</a>)</cite>. These systems aim to promote healthy behavior in sedentary contexts. However, most existing approaches rely on coarse-grained classification strategies (e.g., ”standard posture,” ”leaning forward,” ”reclining”) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yongxiang et al. 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cai et al. 2021</a>)</cite> and simplistic interaction mechanisms such as fixed prompts or audiovisual alerts<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al. 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">La Mura et al. 2023</a>)</cite>. Such designs fall short in real-world scenarios characterized by diverse user needs and complex, dynamically changing posture states.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Current sitting posture recognition systems face two fundamental limitations: (1) insufficient perceptual granularity, which hampers the detection of subtle anomalies in key anatomical regions such as the shoulders, lower back, and pelvis; and (2) lack of semantic feedback, as most systems rely on templated, low-level responses that cannot support personalized recommendations or multi-layered health-oriented dialogues.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Among various sensing modalities, flexible pressure sensors have emerged as a compelling solution for continuous and stable posture monitoring, owing to their non-invasive nature and ease of integration. However, pressure distribution alone lacks the semantic expressiveness necessary for high-level understanding and personalized interaction.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To address the limitations in semantic expressiveness, we introduce a large language model to interpret pressure sensor data and generate personalized responses.Despite recent advances in LLMs’ multi-task semantic understanding and generation capabilities, applying LLMs directly to posture modeling remains challenging due to three core factors:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="Sx1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Sx1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Modality Gap and Semantic Misalignment: Raw pressure maps are inherently low-dimensional and unstructured, lacking explicit semantic annotations<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al. 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deldari et al. 2022</a>)</cite>. This makes it difficult for LLMs to establish meaningful spatial-semantic correspondences, often leading to representation errors and semantic drift.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Limited Alignment Capability &amp; High Deployment Cost: Mainstream LLMs are typically large in scale and computationally expensive, posing difficulties for real-time deployment on resource-limited devices. Meanwhile, smaller models, while efficient, suffer from reduced expressiveness and weakened cross-modal alignment, severely limiting downstream performance<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Domain Knowledge Deficiency: General-purpose LLMs lack specialized knowledge in posture-related health risks, which impedes their ability to identify abnormal postures and generate actionable, clinically relevant feedback.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, we propose SitLLM, a lightweight multimodal framework that integrates flexible pressure sensing with LLM-based semantic modeling to support fine-grained sitting posture understanding and personalized feedback generation. Specifically, our contributions include:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="Sx1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Sx1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gaussian-Robust Sensor Embedding Module:</span> We adopt a ViT-style block-wise encoding strategy with Gaussian perturbation to enhance spatial robustness.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sensor Queries Alignment Module:</span> This module bridges the modality gap by projecting sensor embeddings as queries into the LLM’s vocabulary space through multi-head cross-attention, achieving deep semantic alignment without requiring large-scale paired pretraining.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-Context Prompt Module:</span> We design a hierarchical prompt mechanism that injects feature-level, structure-level, statistical-level, and instruction-level sensor semantics into the LLM. This allows the model to integrate multimodal cues and improve response generation across diverse sitting health tasks.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx1.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Instruction-Tuned Dataset with Medical Knowledge Injection:</span> We construct a fine-grained sensor–text dataset that covers four types of posture-related tasks and 120 typical sitting postures. Our two-stage generation pipeline incorporates expert annotations, retrieval-augmented medical knowledge, and GPT-4o-based semantic generation.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Experimental results demonstrate that our proposed framework significantly outperforms existing methods in representative tasks such as posture description, personalized suggestion generation, and posture-related health Q&amp;A. It achieves superior accuracy, generalization, and semantic interaction capabilities. These findings validate the effectiveness of integrating flexible pressure sensing with lightweight LLMs under resource constraints, and demonstrate the potential of our approach to support real-time, multi-task, health-aware intelligent agents.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Related Work</h2>

<section id="Sx2.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Aligning Sensor with Language</h3>

<div id="Sx2.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although establishing semantic connections between sensor data and natural language has emerged as a growing research focus, constructing large-scale, high-quality, and semantically aligned cross-modal corpora from unstructured sensor signals remains a significant challenge. Such corpora are essential for training high-performance cross-modal models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Imran et al. 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Existing studies generally adopt two main strategies to address this issue. The first involves generating textual summaries of sensor features using fixed template rules or incorporating raw sensor values as structured prompts into downstream LLM-based tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. 2024</a>)</cite>. The second strategy employs dedicated sensor encoders combined with alignment modules to enhance the model’s capacity for interpreting and reasoning over sensor-derived information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Imran et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.SSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, these approaches are fundamentally constrained by the sparsity and limited semantic coverage of available sensor-text paired data, which impedes both the semantic alignment capability and the generalization performance of cross-modal models across diverse tasks.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12994v1/pipeline.png" id="Sx2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/399;" width="698" height="399" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>
Overview of the SitLLM architecture. The framework integrates three core components: (1) the <span id="Sx2.F2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sensor Embedding Module</span>, which transforms pressure maps into spatially-aware embeddings; (2) the <span id="Sx2.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sensor Queries Alignment Module</span>, which reprograms sensor embeddings into the LLM’s vocabulary space through multi-head cross-attention; and (3) the <span id="Sx2.F2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-Context Prompt Module</span>, which injects multi-level semantic contexts into the language model.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="Sx2.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Health LLM</h3>

<div id="Sx2.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In recent years, large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in medical language understanding and reasoning, driving advances in tasks such as intelligent consultation and health-related question answering<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Singhal et al. 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nori et al. 2023</a>)</cite>. Studies on models like PaLM 2 and GPT-4 have shown that domain-specific fine-tuning and instruction tuning can endow LLMs with the ability to outperform traditional methods in various medical benchmarks, exhibiting causal reasoning, personalized generation, and clinical inference.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">With the rise of multimodal learning, researchers have extended LLMs to medical imaging modalities (e.g., X-rays, MRIs), resulting in visual-language models such as Med-Flamingo<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moor et al. 2023</a>)</cite> and LLaVA-Med<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2023a</a>)</cite>. However, these efforts predominantly focus on static images, while the modeling of temporal physiological signals and wearable sensor data remains in its nascent stage.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.SSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recent work has begun to explore the application of LLMs to low-dimensional physiological modalities such as ECG and PPG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feli et al. 2025</a>)</cite>, incorporating prompt engineering and structured prompting mechanisms to enhance semantic alignment. Concurrently, models like Health-LLM<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, LLaSA<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Imran et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, and Sensor2Text<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. 2024</a>)</cite> have expanded the cross-modal capabilities of LLMs in domains such as activity recognition, mental health assessment, and multi-turn health dialogues. These efforts mark a shift from image-centric modeling toward sensor-informed semantic understanding.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.SSx2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Nevertheless, most of these approaches still rely on limited sensor–text paired data and lack a unified modeling framework for encoding structural priors, task types, and semantic preferences. This significantly hinders their generalization and interpretability in complex health scenarios involving dynamic behavior, personalized feedback, and semantic reasoning.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Model Structure</h2>

<div id="Sx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SitLLM is composed of three key modules:(1) Sensor Embedding Module, which encodes pressure modality data into compact and structure-aware feature vectors;(2) Sensor Queries Alignment Module, which aligns sensor embeddings with the textual semantic space, enabling deep semantic connectivity with the LLM;(3) Multi-Context Prompt Module, which injects sensor semantic information into the backbone language model through multi-level contextual prompting to generate the final response.An overview of the overall architecture is shown in Figure <a href="#Sx2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Aligning Sensor with Language ‣ Related Work ‣ SitLLM: Large Language Models for Sitting Posture Health Understanding via Pressure Sensor Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx3.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Sensor Embedding Module</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt a Vision Transformer (ViT)-style encoding strategy to process the input pressure map. Given a two-dimensional pressure matrix <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times W}</annotation></semantics></math>, we first divide it into fixed-size patches <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{s\times s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{s\times s}</annotation></semantics></math>. To enhance the model’s robustness against sensor noise and subtle posture perturbations, we introduce a Gaussian perturbation mechanism during training, adding random noise to each patch:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="Sx3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}^{\prime}=p_{i}+\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}I)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}^{\prime}=p_{i}+\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}I)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is a tunable hyperparameter controlling the intensity of the Gaussian noise. Each perturbed patch is then flattened and passed through a linear projection to obtain its embedding representation <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. To retain spatial positional information, a learnable positional encoding <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\text{pos}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>pos</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\text{pos}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> is added. The final embedding sequence is then obtained by:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="Sx3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\text{Transformer}(\text{Concat}(e_{i}+E_{\text{pos}}))\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Transformer</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>Concat</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>pos</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\text{Transformer}(\text{Concat}(e_{i}+E_{\text{pos}}))\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Sx3.SSx1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=\frac{H\times W}{s^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=\frac{H\times W}{s^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of patches. The Transformer encoder is used to model both local and long-range spatial dependencies.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Sensor Queries Alignment Module</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Due to the lack of available aligned pre-trained models for sensor modalities, directly mapping sensor representations into the language space remains a significant challenge. We propose an unsupervised reprogramming mechanism that treats the sensor embeddings <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> as queries and projects them into the LLM’s pretrained vocabulary embedding space <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\in\mathbb{R}^{V\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\in\mathbb{R}^{V\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the vocabulary size. Cross-modal semantic alignment is achieved through a multi-head cross-attention mechanism. For the <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th attention head, the projections are defined as:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="Sx3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{k}=XW_{k}^{Q},\quad K_{k}=EW_{k}^{K},\quad V_{k}=EW_{k}^{V}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>Q</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>K</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>V</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{k}=XW_{k}^{Q},\quad K_{k}=EW_{k}^{K},\quad V_{k}=EW_{k}^{V}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{k}^{Q},W_{k}^{K},W_{k}^{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>Q</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{k}^{Q},W_{k}^{K},W_{k}^{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> are learnable projection matrices. The attention output for head <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is computed as:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="Sx3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{k}=\text{Attention}(Q_{k},K_{k},V_{k})=\text{Softmax}\left(\frac{Q_{k}K_{k}^{\top}}{\sqrt{d}}\right)V_{k}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Attention</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{k}=\text{Attention}(Q_{k},K_{k},V_{k})=\text{Softmax}\left(\frac{Q_{k}K_{k}^{\top}}{\sqrt{d}}\right)V_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The outputs of all heads are concatenated to obtain the fused representation:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="Sx3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=\text{Concat}(Z_{1},Z_{2},...,Z_{K})\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Concat</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=\text{Concat}(Z_{1},Z_{2},...,Z_{K})\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is projected through a linear transformation, followed by normalization and dropout, to match the hidden dimension <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> of the LLM backbone, yielding the aligned features <math id="Sx3.SSx2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times D}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Multi-context Prompt Module</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prompting is the most direct approach to injecting modality-specific knowledge into large language models (LLMs). In multimodal LLMs, it is common to wrap the encoded image features with pad tokens and insert them as a prefix before the textual input to guide generation<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al. 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2023b</a>)</cite>. However, for sensor modalities, the absence of large-scale aligned training data leads to incomplete semantic representations, making simple prefix concatenation insufficient to effectively activate the reasoning capability of LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this issue, we design a multi-context prompt mechanism that incorporates multi-level semantic cues from the sensor modality, including:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Feature-level context: Preserves the raw semantic features of the sensor modality by directly using the aligned embeddings as token-level prompts, guiding the model to attend to localized perceptual information.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Structure-level context: Encodes spatial layout and dynamic characteristics of the sensor array, such as sensor positions, distribution density, sampling intervals, and resolution.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Statistical-level context: Extracts global statistical summaries (e.g., maximum pressure, minimum pressure, mean, variance), enabling the model to build a high-level understanding of the input.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Semantic-level context primarily encodes the user’s task instructions, explicitly guiding the language model to comprehend the current task intent. It works in conjunction with the aforementioned feature-level, structure-level, and statistical-level sensor semantics to generate responses that align with the instruction semantics.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">These contextual cues are fused with natural language instructions through prompt engineering, forming a composite input that guides the backbone LLM to effectively leverage supplemental semantic information from the sensor modality during instruction interpretation. Compared to conventional single-level prompting, the multi-context prompt structure offers more efficient and comprehensive representation, effectively mitigating performance bottlenecks caused by insufficient cross-modal alignment.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Sensor-Language Dataset Construction</h2>

<figure id="Sx4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12994v1/data_create.png" id="Sx4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/393;" width="698" height="393" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>
Overview of our two-stage sensor–language dataset construction pipeline.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Sx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Existing datasets often suffer from coarse-grained classification<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clever et al. 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan and Li 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gelaw and Hagos 2021</a>)</cite>, lacking fine-grained aligned textual descriptions corresponding to pressure sensor data, and missing professional medical knowledge related to sitting posture health. These limitations restrict the model’s ability to perform fine-grained understanding of sitting posture features represented by pressure data, hinder its generalization across various sitting health instruction tasks, and limit its professionalism in addressing medical health-related question answering tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address these issues, we propose a sensor–text alignment construction method that incorporates professional medical health knowledge. Based on this method, we introduce medical knowledge datasets such as HuaTuo-26M<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2023c</a>)</cite> to build a high-quality sitting pressure–text alignment dataset containing 9,255 samples, covering four task types, and roughly categorized into 120 posture types. An overview of this process is illustrated in Figure <a href="#Sx4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Sensor-Language Dataset Construction ‣ SitLLM: Large Language Models for Sitting Posture Health Understanding via Pressure Sensor Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The dataset includes not only fine-grained posture descriptions (e.g., the support condition of the backrest on the upper left back, the degree of contact between the right thigh and the seat surface), but also various types of sitting health tasks, such as posture problem analysis, posture correction suggestions, and sitting health question answering.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our proposed construction method consists of two stages.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx4.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Stage 1</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First, we recruited eight volunteers with diverse heights and body types to collect a total of 1,063 valid pressure samples under various sitting postures. During data collection, three experienced experts simultaneously provided fine-grained descriptive annotations for each posture, producing high-quality textual representations. Based on variations across different body regions, these samples can be categorized into approximately 120 typical sitting postures, forming a solid foundation for subsequent task-aligned data construction.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Second, we built a structured knowledge base covering disease types potentially caused by improper sitting postures. Based on this knowledge base, we screened relevant instruction-tuning (SFT) samples from medical datasets such as HuaTuo-26M<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2023c</a>)</cite>, DISC-Med-SFT<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bao et al. 2023</a>)</cite>, and FreedomIntelligence/HuatuoGPT2-SFT-GPT4-140K<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. 2023</a>)</cite>. Using the all-MiniLM-L6-v2<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2020</a>)</cite> model, we encoded the instruction texts into semantic vectors to construct a retrievable sitting posture health knowledge base, which was later used to inject professional medical knowledge into our dataset.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Stage 2</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the second stage, we employed GPT-4o to automatically generate pressure–text alignment samples. Specifically, we first randomly sampled an expert-annotated pressure instance and its corresponding posture description as the input example, prompting the model to generate a task-specific question (e.g., posture analysis, health recommendation). We then encoded this question into a semantic vector and retrieved the Top10 most relevant knowledge entries from the medical knowledge base based on cosine similarity. Among these, five entries were randomly selected as domain knowledge prompts. These, along with the original posture description, were embedded into the prompt to guide GPT-4o in generating three candidate responses.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate response quality, we designed a scoring prompt that guided the model to assign scores from two aspects: accuracy and relevance, yielding an LLM-based score <math id="Sx4.SSx2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{llm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>llm</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{llm}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Additionally, we employed keyword extraction to identify sitting posture features in the responses and compared them against those in the original description, computing both keyword coverage and semantic similarity to obtain a feature score <math id="Sx4.SSx2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{feat}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>feat</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{feat}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This metric reflects whether irrelevant or incorrect features were introduced.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we combined the two scores using a weighted sum to compute the final score:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="Sx4.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Sx4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathrm{final}}=\alpha\cdot S_{\mathrm{llm}}+(1-\alpha)\cdot S_{\mathrm{feat}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>final</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>llm</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>feat</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathrm{final}}=\alpha\cdot S_{\mathrm{llm}}+(1-\alpha)\cdot S_{\mathrm{feat}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">and selected the highest-scoring response to be included in the final sitting pressure–text alignment dataset.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Parameter Efficient Training</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To mitigate catastrophic forgetting and improve training efficiency, we adopt a parameter-efficient fine-tuning strategy to optimize the pre-trained large language model (LLM). Specifically, we incorporate Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. 2022</a>)</cite> into the backbone model to enable lightweight tuning of key modules, while applying full-parameter fine-tuning to other trainable components in a joint optimization manner. During training, the primary objective is the supervised loss <math id="Sx4.SSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{sup}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>sup</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{sup}}</annotation></semantics></math>, computed between the model’s output and the reference answer.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Experiments</h2>

<figure id="Sx5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="Sx5.T1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="Sx5.T1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
