<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Cross-model evaluation on pressure-sensor Q&amp;A tasks. Results for Analysis, Correction, Description, and Question tasks are reported across four metrics: BLEU, ROUGE-L (R-L), BERTScore (BERT), and METEOR.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="Sx5.SSx3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Dataset.</h4>

<div id="Sx5.SSx3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.SSx3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the model’s capabilities across multiple core tasks in the sitting posture health domain, including fine-grained posture perception, posture risk analysis, correction suggestion generation, and health-related question answering. To this end, we construct an evaluation dataset comprising 1,000 samples, collected from two individuals who did not participate in the training process, ensuring objectivity and generalization of the testing phase. The construction of the evaluation data strictly follows the same filtering criteria and generation pipeline as the pre-training dataset, covering four representative task categories with 250 samples per task.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx5.SSx3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Implementation Details.</h4>

<div id="Sx5.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team 2024</a>)</cite> as the backbone large language model and perform parameter-efficient fine-tuning using the LoRA technique, with the rank parameter set to <math id="Sx5.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=64</annotation></semantics></math> and the scaling factor <math id="Sx5.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{lora}_{\alpha}=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>lora</mtext><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{lora}_{\alpha}=16</annotation></semantics></math>. The optimization is conducted using the Adam optimizer<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma and Ba 2014</a>)</cite> with an initial learning rate of <math id="Sx5.SSx3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>. The model is trained for 5 epochs with a batch size of 4, gradient accumulation steps of 8, and a maximum sequence length of 4096.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx5.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Generation Quality Evaluation</h3>

<section id="Sx5.SSx1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines.</h4>

<div id="Sx5.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Currently, there is no unified evaluation benchmark for posture-related question answering (QA) tasks based on pressure sensor data. To address this gap, we designed an evaluation protocol that leverages the prompt engineering capabilities of large language models (LLMs) to construct QA tasks. Specifically, we inject pressure-sensing information into the model through structured prompts, including key attributes such as sensor placement, value range, sampling precision, and the spatial intervals between pressure points. Additionally, raw pressure data is encoded into textual form and embedded within the prompt to guide the model in understanding pressure distribution for task completion. We conduct benchmark testing across several representative LLMs, including the open-source models Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct, DeepSeek-V3 and DeepSeek-R1, the proprietary models GPT-4o, Qwen-Max, and Gemini 2.5 Pro, as well as the lightweight backbone model Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct, which serves as the foundation of our system.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx5.SSx1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Metric.</h4>

<div id="Sx5.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We employ a variety of mainstream natural language processing evaluation metrics to assess the quality of the model-generated outputs. Specifically, BLEU<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Papineni et al. 2002</a>)</cite>, ROUGE-L<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin 2004</a>)</cite>, and METEOR<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Banerjee and Lavie 2005</a>)</cite> are used as fundamental metrics to measure surface-level similarity and n-gram overlap between the generated text and reference answers. Additionally, to further evaluate semantic consistency, we incorporate BertScore<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2019</a>)</cite>, which leverages a pretrained BERT model to compute the cosine similarity between contextual embeddings of the generated and reference texts, thereby effectively capturing deeper semantic matching.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx5.SSx1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Results.</h4>

<div id="Sx5.SSx1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.SSx1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Detailed evaluation results for all models are presented in <a href="#Sx5.T1" title="In Experiments ‣ SitLLM: Large Language Models for Sitting Posture Health Understanding via Pressure Sensor Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Notably, compared to the strongest general-purpose LLM, GPT-4o, our SitLLM-1.5B achieves significant improvements of 56.5% and 44.5% in BLEU and METEOR scores, respectively, on the core <span id="Sx5.SSx1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Analysis</span> task. When compared to a lightweight model of the same scale, Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct, we observe an overall performance gain of over 83.7% across all tasks. Furthermore, in both the <span id="Sx5.SSx1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correction</span> and <span id="Sx5.SSx1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question</span> tasks, our method surpasses all baseline models, achieving key metric advantages ranging from 10.5% to 18.6%.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx5.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Semantic Quality Evaluation</h3>

<section id="Sx5.SSx2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines.</h4>

<div id="Sx5.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.SSx2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We perform semantic quality evaluation using the same baseline models and prompt-based generation methodology as adopted in the Generation Quality Evaluation section.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx5.T2" class="ltx_table">
