<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Semantic alignment performance across four sitting posture health tasks, evaluated using GPT-Score (G-S) and expert Human-Score (H-S).</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="Sx5.SSx2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Metric.</h4>

<div id="Sx5.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt both automated LLM-based metrics (GPT-Score) and expert human evaluations (Human-Score), guided by a unified, domain-specific rubric.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx5.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.SSx2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">GPT-Score utilizes GPT-4o as an expert evaluator via a customized prompt tailored to the sitting posture health domain. The model assesses the alignment between generated and reference answers by analyzing key domain-relevant concepts such as affected body regions, pressure distribution patterns, posture-related health risks, and correction strategies. Scores are assigned on a continuous scale from 0 to 1, based on semantic accuracy, content coverage, and the presence of critical domain-specific terminology. This follows the established ”LLM-as-a-judge” paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. 2023</a>)</cite>.
Human-Score is produced by posture health experts using the same rubric as the GPT-based evaluator. Experts rate responses on a 0–1 scale, ensuring consistency in evaluation criteria while incorporating professional domain knowledge.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx5.SSx2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Results.</h4>

<div id="Sx5.SSx2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.SSx2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#Sx5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Baselines. ‣ Semantic Quality Evaluation ‣ Experiments ‣ SitLLM: Large Language Models for Sitting Posture Health Understanding via Pressure Sensor Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows that SitLLM-1.5B achieves the best semantic alignment across all tasks, outperforming strong baselines including GPT-4o and Qwen2.5-72B by up to 3.8% in GPT-Score and 4.0% in Human-Score. Gains are especially notable in Correction and Question tasks, evidencing superior domain understanding and response accuracy. High consistency between GPT-Score and Human-Score validates the effectiveness of our automated evaluation.These results demonstrate that our domain-specific fine-tuning and prompt design effectively enhance the model’s semantic understanding and alignment with expert knowledge, thus enabling more accurate and clinically meaningful posture health assessments.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<table id="Sx5.T3.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="Sx5.T3.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="Sx5.T3.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">
<span id="Sx5.T3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:505.9pt;">
<span id="Sx5.T3.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx5.T3.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="Sx5.T3.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="Sx5.T3.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">
<span id="Sx5.T3.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:505.9pt;">
<span id="Sx5.T3.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx5.T3.1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">I feel a lot of pressure in the front of my thighs when I sit. Will this affect blood circulation in my legs and possibly cause varicose veins or numbness in my lower limbs? How can I prevent these problems?</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="Sx5.T3.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="Sx5.T3.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">
<span id="Sx5.T3.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:505.9pt;">
<span id="Sx5.T3.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx5.T3.1.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-4o Answer</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="Sx5.T3.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="Sx5.T3.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">
<span id="Sx5.T3.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:505.9pt;">
<span id="Sx5.T3.1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">The increased pressure on the front of the thighs can indeed affect blood circulation in the legs. Maintaining this posture for long periods of time can lead to varicose veins or leg numbness. To prevent these problems, it’s recommended to adjust your sitting posture to distribute pressure and avoid excessive pressure on a single area. You can also use a more ergonomic chair and increase regular movement and stretching exercises to improve circulation. Regularly standing up and moving around, changing your posture every hour, and avoiding sitting in the same position for extended periods can help reduce leg discomfort and prevent related problems.</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="Sx5.T3.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="Sx5.T3.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">
<span id="Sx5.T3.1.5.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:505.9pt;">
<span id="Sx5.T3.1.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx5.T3.1.5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SitLLM-1.5B Answer</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="Sx5.T3.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="Sx5.T3.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">
<span id="Sx5.T3.1.6.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:505.9pt;">
<span id="Sx5.T3.1.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Yes, excessive pressure on the front of the thighs may affect blood circulation in the legs, increasing the risk of varicose veins or leg numbness. <span id="Sx5.T3.1.6.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">You put too much weight on the front edge of the seat cushion, and your legs are under great pressure.</span> This sitting posture compresses the blood vessels in the thigh area, resulting in poor blood return. Over time, it may cause varicose veins in the lower limbs or leg numbness. To prevent these problems, you first need to adjust your sitting posture. <span id="Sx5.T3.1.6.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Make sure your entire buttocks are sitting deep on the chair, avoid putting your center of gravity on the front edge of the seat cushion.</span> At the same time, <span id="Sx5.T3.1.6.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">keep your back in a normal curvature and avoid leaning back too much.</span> You can consider <span id="Sx5.T3.1.6.1.1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">adjusting the height of the chair and the tilt angle of the seat cushion to keep the knee and hip joints at about 90 degrees.</span> In addition, get up and move around every 30 minutes to promote blood circulation in the lower limbs. If your work requires long periods of sitting, you can stretch your leg muscles regularly or gently shake your ankles.</span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Case study comparing GPT-4o and SitLLM in answering a posture-related health question. SitLLM provides more personalized and structured recommendations, with key points marked in <span id="Sx5.T3.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">red</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="Sx5.T4" class="ltx_table">
<table id="Sx5.T4.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="Sx5.T4.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="Sx5.T4.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding:-1pt 4.0pt;"><span id="Sx5.T4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics</span></th>
<th id="Sx5.T4.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:-1pt 4.0pt;"><span id="Sx5.T4.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct</span></th>
<th id="Sx5.T4.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:-1pt 4.0pt;"><span id="Sx5.T4.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct</span></th>
<th id="Sx5.T4.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding:-1pt 4.0pt;"><span id="Sx5.T4.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SitLLM-1.5B</span></th></tr>
</thead>
