<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Zero-shot learning performance comparison on the <span id="Sx5.T4.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">question</span> task. Results show SitLLM’s cross-task transfer capability when trained only on <span id="Sx5.T4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">description</span>, <span id="Sx5.T4.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">analysis</span>, and <span id="Sx5.T4.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">correction</span> tasks.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx5.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Zero-shot Learning</h3>

<section id="Sx5.SSx3.SSS0.Px1a" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Setup.</h4>

<div id="Sx5.SSx3.SSS0.Px1a.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.SSx3.SSS0.Px1a.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models typically exhibit strong zero-shot task generalization capabilities. To evaluate whether SitLLM demonstrates similar performance on unseen sitting posture health tasks, we designed a cross-task generalization experiment. Specifically, SitLLM is trained only on data from three task types: <span id="Sx5.SSx3.SSS0.Px1a.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">description</span>, <span id="Sx5.SSx3.SSS0.Px1a.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">analysis</span>, and <span id="Sx5.SSx3.SSS0.Px1a.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">correction</span>, using the same training configurations and hyperparameters as the full-data setting. Without accessing any training data from the <span id="Sx5.SSx3.SSS0.Px1a.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">question</span> task, we evaluate SitLLM on its corresponding test set to assess its zero-shot generalization performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx5.SSx3.SSS0.Px2a" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines.</h4>

<div id="Sx5.SSx3.SSS0.Px2a.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.SSx3.SSS0.Px2a.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To comprehensively evaluate the zero-shot task generalization capability of SitLLM, we conduct a comparative analysis against two baseline models: Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct, which is of a similar scale, and Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct, which achieves the best performance on the <span id="Sx5.SSx3.SSS0.Px2a.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">question</span> task.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx5.SSx3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Metric.</h4>

<div id="Sx5.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.SSx3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To comprehensively evaluate the zero-shot generalization ability of SitLLM, we adopt both the standard natural language generation metrics used in the Generation Quality Evaluation (BLEU, ROUGE-L, BERTScore, and METEOR) and the semantic quality metrics from the Semantic Quality Evaluation (GPT-Score and Human-Score).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx5.SSx3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Results.</h4>

<div id="Sx5.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#Sx5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Results. ‣ Semantic Quality Evaluation ‣ Experiments ‣ SitLLM: Large Language Models for Sitting Posture Health Understanding via Pressure Sensor Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, SitLLM-1.5B demonstrates strong zero-shot generalization ability on the unseen <span id="Sx5.SSx3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">question</span> task, even without access to any question-specific training data. Compared to Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct, SitLLM achieves superior performance across all six evaluation metrics, with notable improvements in both generation quality and semantic quality. While Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct benefits from significantly larger model capacity, SitLLM-1.5B still outperforms it on key generation metrics such as BLEU and GPT-Score.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx5.SSx4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Case Study</h3>

<div id="Sx5.SSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.SSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To qualitatively assess the effectiveness of SitLLM, we conducted a comparative analysis against GPT-4o in the context of real-world sitting posture health scenarios (Table <a href="#Sx5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Results. ‣ Semantic Quality Evaluation ‣ Experiments ‣ SitLLM: Large Language Models for Sitting Posture Health Understanding via Pressure Sensor Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). While GPT-4o tends to provide general health suggestions, SitLLM demonstrates a finer-grained understanding of user posture by explicitly referencing pressure patterns sensed from the seat (e.g., excessive pressure on the front edge of the cushion). Based on this sensory insight, SitLLM generates targeted correction suggestions, such as adjusting hip positioning, modifying lumbar support, or shifting weight distribution and seat angle. These results highlight SitLLM’s capacity to deliver more fine-grained, personalized, and expert-level responses by leveraging multimodal pressure data for sitting posture reasoning and health guidance.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Conclusion</h2>

<div id="Sx6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We presented SitLLM, a lightweight multimodal framework that integrates pressure sensing with large language models for sitting posture understanding and health feedback. By introducing robust sensor embedding, prompt-based semantic alignment, and structured instruction injection, SitLLM achieves accurate, interpretable, and task-adaptive posture analysis. To support training and evaluation, we constructed a high-quality multimodal dataset with diverse pressure–text pairs. Extensive experiments across four tasks confirm the effectiveness of SitLLM under low-resource settings and its superiority over strong baselines. Future work will explore the integration of diverse sensor modalities and their application to broader health-related scenarios beyond posture, including intelligent medical monitoring and assistive interventions.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
